  "candidate_0109": {
    "reason": "The candidate asserts that complement proteins bind to synaptic sites to mark them for elimination, thereby regulating the density of new neuronal connections. The provided references (candidate_0106 and candidate_0107) establish that astrocytes secrete thrombospondins which bind to neuronal receptors to influence connection formation/density. Reference candidate_0108 states that astrocytes release complement proteins but does not specify the mechanism of binding to synaptic sites, the function of marking for elimination, or how this specifically regulates density via elimination. The references describe a regulatory process involving complement proteins but do not entail the specific mechanism (marking for elimination) or the functional outcome (regulating density via elimination) claimed","additional_information": ["Complement proteins bind to synaptic sites", "This binding marks synaptic sites for elimination", "This process regulates the density of new neuronal connections"], "status": "new", "evidence_ids": []
  }
